<section aria-labelledby="load">
<h2 id="load">Load generation: open and closed loop</h2>
<dl class="defs">
<dt>Open loop</dt>
<dd>{{ open_loop|sentence }}. Requests are sent on the arrival schedule whatever the server does, so queueing shows up as latency. This is the mode used for latency SLOs and goodput; load is in requests per second.</dd>
<dt>Closed loop</dt>
<dd>{{ closed_loop|sentence }}. The server never sees more than that many requests in flight, so it measures saturation throughput, and the highest concurrency that meets the SLO is the maximum sustainable concurrency.</dd>
</dl>
<p>Warmup requests are discarded before any metric is computed. On cloud runs the load generator runs on the GPU host itself, so client-observed latency never includes a round trip over the internet. Each run is one load point × one repetition, with its seed, workload profile and dataset recorded in its provenance.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="metrics">
<h2 id="metrics">Latency and throughput</h2>
<dl class="defs">
<dt>TTFT</dt><dd>Time to first token: from sending the request to receiving the first streamed token.</dd>
<dt>TPOT</dt><dd>Time per output token after the first: (end-to-end latency − TTFT) ÷ (output tokens − 1), per request.</dd>
<dt>Error rate</dt><dd>(errors + timeouts) ÷ (successful + errors + timeouts) requests.</dd>
<dt>Throughput</dt><dd>Tokens of successful requests ÷ the measurement window. Token counts come from the server's usage block; a successful request without one is counted and flagged, and its tokens are left out.</dd>
<dt>Raw peak</dt><dd>The highest output throughput at any tested load, ignoring the SLO. It is reported for context and never used for cost.</dd>
</dl>
<p>Latency percentiles are computed per run over successful requests. A run's p95 TTFT is one value; repetitions of the same load point are then combined as described under <a href="#ci">confidence intervals</a>.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="goodput">
<h2 id="goodput">Goodput at SLO</h2>
<p>An SLO sets latency targets (for example p95 TTFT and p95 TPOT in milliseconds) and a maximum error rate. Each experiment declares its SLO; the one used for published results is under <a href="#snapshot">this snapshot</a>.</p>
<ol>
<li>A load point <strong>meets the SLO</strong> only if, for every target, the upper bound of its confidence interval across repetitions is within the target. Being under the target on average is not enough.</li>
<li><strong>Goodput</strong> is the highest load that meets the SLO below the first load that does not. Loads above the first failure never count, even if they happen to pass.</li>
<li>Goodput throughput (output and input tokens per second, per replica) is the mean measured at that load, with its confidence interval.</li>
<li>If every tested load met the SLO, goodput is not bracketed: the real goodput may be higher, the cost is an upper bound, and the result is flagged and not ranked.</li>
</ol>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="ci">
<h2 id="ci">Repetitions and confidence intervals</h2>
<p>{{ method.ci_method }}</p>
{% call scroll_table("ci-methods-h") %}
<table>
<caption id="ci-methods-h">Interval method by metric</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Metrics</th><th scope="col">Point estimate and interval</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
{% for row in method.ci_methods %}
<tr><td>{{ row.metrics|sentence }}</td><td>{{ row.method|sentence }}</td></tr>
{% endfor %}
</tbody>
</table>
{% endcall %}
<p>Why the log scale: with two or three repetitions, a symmetric interval around the mean of a noisy latency or throughput routinely reaches below zero, a value the quantity cannot take, and then makes the cost interval unbounded. The log-scale interval respects positivity and is wide in the direction the data are uncertain: a factor, not a fixed number of milliseconds.</p>
<p>A result is <strong>untrusted</strong>, listed but not ranked, when any load point has fewer than two completed repetitions, goodput is not bracketed, a headline metric at the goodput load varies run to run by more than 10% (coefficient of variation), or successful requests lacked token usage. Runs that did not complete are excluded and counted.</p>
<p>Repetitions in this snapshot: {{ method.repetitions }}.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="cost">
<h2 id="cost">Cost per token</h2>
<p>A replica's hourly price buys the tokens it produces per hour at goodput. A GPU produces input (prefill) and output (decode) tokens at the same time, so splitting its cost between them is a choice, and every result states which:</p>
<dl class="defs">
<dt>all_output</dt><dd>{{ allocation_text['all_output']|sentence }}.</dd>
<dt>all_input</dt><dd>{{ allocation_text['all_input']|sentence }}.</dd>
<dt>weighted(r)</dt><dd>One output token costs as much as r input tokens. With effective throughput E = input tok/s + r × output tok/s, the input price is hourly price ÷ (E × 3,600) per token and the output price is r times that; at the measured token mix these prices bill exactly the replica's hourly cost.</dd>
</dl>
<div class="formula" role="group" aria-label="Cost formula">
<p>$/1M output tokens at SLO = hourly price ÷ (goodput output tok/s × {{ seconds_per_hour|num }} s/h) × {{ tokens_per_mtok|num }}</p>
</div>
<h3 id="price-columns">Which hourly price</h3>
<p>Every hourly price is the replica's instance price plus its block storage volume (EBS gp3) amortised per hour at 730 hours per month, computed the same way in every table, file and report. Network egress and fixed account costs (bucket, secrets, the reaper) are not included.</p>
<dl class="defs">
<dt>On-demand (ranked)</dt><dd>The public on-demand list price from the <a href="#prices">price book</a>. It does not depend on when or where a run launched, so anyone can recompute it: leaderboards, the headline table and margins use it. A mock or local host has no list price and uses the price its experiment declares; without one it has no cost at SLO and is not ranked.</dd>
<dt>Spot</dt><dd>The price book's indicative spot price, an average across availability zones that moves hourly.</dd>
<dt>Committed 1y</dt><dd>A one-year commitment price, shown only where the price book has one.</dd>
<dt>As run</dt><dd>What the host cost per hour when it ran: the spot price observed in its availability zone at launch, or the on-demand price for an on-demand host, recorded with its basis in every run's provenance. The budget guard accrues spot hosts at that price times a safety multiplier, so recorded spend stays above the bill; the multiplier never appears in a reported cost.</dd>
</dl>
<p>Money is held as integer micro-dollars and computed exactly, rounded once, half up, to the micro-dollar. Cost falls as throughput rises, so the low end of the cost interval comes from the high end of the throughput interval and vice versa.</p>

<h3 id="example">Worked example</h3>
{% if example is none %}
<p>No price book is included in this snapshot, so no example can be computed.</p>
{% else %}
{% if example.measured %}
